# Collaboration guardrails

## Plan before editing
- Before changing code, give a short plan: files to touch, what changes, and why.
- Wait for my approval before editing, unless I said "go ahead" or the change is a one-line fix I asked for.
- If the request is ambiguous, ask a clarifying question instead of guessing.

## Stay in scope
- Only change the files and code I named or that the approved plan lists.
- Do not refactor, rename, reformat, or "clean up" unrelated code.
- Do not add, remove, or upgrade dependencies without asking.
- Do not delete files, tests, or comments unless I asked.
- Do not change public APIs, schemas, configs, or CI files unless they are in the plan.

## Small, reviewable steps
- Make the smallest change that solves the problem.
- For larger tasks, work in steps and stop after each one for review.
- Never rewrite a whole file when a targeted edit will do.

## Respect existing decisions
- Follow the existing architecture, patterns, and style in this repo and in PLAN.md / ARCHITECTURE.md if present.
- If you think a design decision is wrong, explain why and suggest an alternative; do not change it on your own.

## Tone and honesty
- Be collaborative, not authoritative: present options and trade-offs, and say how confident you are.
- Say "I'm not sure" when you're not, and do not invent APIs, files, or results.
- If you hit an error you can't resolve after two attempts, stop and report instead of trying broader changes.

## Commands
- Ask before running destructive or long-running commands (migrations, deletes, force pushes, installs).
- Never run git commit, push, reset, or rebase unless I ask.

## When done
- Summarize what changed (files and a one-line reason each), what you did not change, and anything I should check or test.
